    13	# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant D9/2 + D16/0
    15	#
    16	# a) Concrete behavior + trace
    17	# Base task behavior:
    18	# - one or two 8-connected frame pieces already show the LR-symmetric rectangle;
    19	# - every other piece is scattered arbitrarily and can only be assembled by shape,
    20	#   symmetry, and edge-anchor constraints.
    21	# Combo behavior:
    22	# - the frame still stays intact as an aligned HxW outline block;
    23	# - every non-frame piece is moved into a separate arrangement area on the right;
    24	# - pieces whose solved top-left cells share the same output row are placed on the
    25	#   same input row band;
    26	# - within each row band, pieces are ordered left-to-right by their solved column;
    27	# - the arrangement uses global row/column bands, so a piece that belongs near
    28	#   output row i and column j also appears near arrangement row i and column j.
    29	# Small trace:
    30	# - suppose two pieces belong to output row 1, with left piece at output col 0 and
    31	#   right piece at output col 3. In the base task they may appear anywhere.
    32	# - here they are placed in the same input row band, with the left piece visibly to
    33	#   the left of the right piece. A piece from output row 4 is placed on a lower band.
    34	# - solver therefore keeps per-instance input origins and enforces row/column order
    35	#   instead of discarding positions after extracting components.
    36	#
    37	# b) Complexity calibration
    38	# The new clue is strong but not trivial:
    39	# - frame detection, LR symmetry, corner/bar/side anchors, and no-same-color-touch
    40	#   are unchanged;
    41	# - non-frame pieces are still disconnected and must be packed densely;
    42	# - the layout cue is discrete and repeated: several pieces share each row band and
    43	#   several columns recur across rows, so humans can notice the pattern quickly.
    44	# We also reject generators where row structure is too weak, e.g. only one row band
    45	# or every row containing only one piece.
    46	#
    47	# c) Implicit-assumption audit
    48	# Original main() assumed:
    49	# - identical pieces can be grouped into types and lose their input positions;
    50	# - only geometry and symmetry matter after extraction.
    51	# Those assumptions break here because input placement is now informative.
    52	# The combo solver therefore:
    53	# - keeps each non-frame component as its own piece instance;
    54	# - stores each piece bbox top-left input coordinates;
    55	# - compares candidate placements using pairwise row/column sign relations, with
    56	#   equality meaning "same row band" or "same column band".
    57	# Frame logic still works because the generator preserves the base-task frame group
    58	# exactly and keeps non-frame pieces outside that frame bbox.
    59	#
    60	# d) Placement legality
    61	# Generator legality guarantees:
    62	# - row/column bands are sized by the largest piece in that band plus one blank gap,
    63	#   so arranged pieces never overlap;
    64	# - frame and arrangement are pasted into disjoint rectangles with fixed separation;
    65	# - all placements stay in-bounds because the canvas size is derived from patch sizes.
    66	#
    67	# e) Visual observability
    68	# A human sees two independent but cooperative cues:
    69	# - the left block already outlines the final symmetric rectangle;
    70	# - the right block is an exploded table of pieces, clearly grouped into horizontal
    71	#   rows that mirror solved row positions and ordered columns that mirror solved
    72	#   left/right positions.
    73	# This is visible from a few examples without relying on color ids or hidden state.
    74	#
    75	# f) Visual cleanliness
    76	# The frame and the arranged pieces occupy separate regions. Inside the arrangement
    77	# region, blank separators isolate row bands and column bands. This avoids mixing the
    78	# contour cue with the row-major cue in the same pixels.
    79	#
    80	# g) Change inferability
    81	# No new color transformation is introduced. Colors retain their original role; the
    82	# only new information is spatial. That keeps inference burden on visible structure,
    83	# not on arbitrary palette remappings.
    84	#
    85	# h) Variant metric
    86	# We measure three scores on generated samples:
    87	# - row_rank_score: piece input-row-band rank matches solved row-band rank;
    88	# - col_rank_score: piece input-column-band rank matches solved column-band rank;
    89	# - pairwise_score: all pairwise row/column order signs match.
    90	# Intended score for each metric is 1.0 on every accepted sample.
    91	#
    92	# i) New main() assumptions and generator guarantees
    93	# Solver assumptions introduced by the combo:
    94	# - non-frame pieces preserve solved row/column partial order in the input;
    95	# - equal solved row starts become equal input row-band starts;
    96	# - equal solved column starts become equal input column-band starts.
    97	# Generator guarantees them by building the arrangement area from monotone row/column
    98	# maps keyed by the true output bbox top-left coordinates of each non-frame piece.
    99	# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
